  "findings": [
    {
      "evidence": "scripts/rollout_project.py parse_runner_labels: `inner = value[1:-1] if value.startswith(\"[\") and value.endswith(\"]\") else value` followed by `inner.split(\",\")`. The split applies to both the list and non-list cases. The `custom` filter only treats a value as default when `group(1).strip(\"'\\\"\") == DEFAULT_RUNNER`, so the bracketed `[ubuntu-latest]` is not recognised as the default. The tests cover neither case. The fix is to reject a comma in a non-bracketed value, and to decide explicitly what `[ubuntu-latest]` means.",
      "id": "unbracketed-comma-runs-on-misparsed",
      "severity": "advisory",
      "summary": "parse_runner_labels splits an unbracketed plain scalar on commas, so `runs-on: a, b` is treated as two labels, but YAML reads it as the single string \"a, b\". The same happens for `[ubuntu-latest]`: it counts as a custom runner and migrates to self-hosted. The migration could record answers that differ from what GitHub was actually using."
    }
  ],
